“McLeod syndrome”), and sporadic forms of chorea-acantho-
cytosis (ChAc) or neuroacanthocytosis have all been reported,
the clinical phenotypes are very similar in all of them.
1 Re-
cently, a novel gene (named CHAC) responsible for autosomal
recessive ChAc (AR-ChAc) has been identiﬁed.
2– 4
Typical movement disorders associated with ChAc are cho-
reiform movements, deﬁned as random, irregular, involuntary
movements of the trunk and limbs.
